    <DataNode elementId="AT5G48230-MONOMER" textLabel="acetoacetyl-Co A thiolase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="AT5G48230-MONOMER" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="775" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">AACT2 has acetoacetyl-CoA thiolase activity based on its ability to complement a yeast &amp;Delta;&lt;i&gt;erg10&lt;/i&gt; mutant and &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays using recombinant protein |CITS: [22332816]|. Compared to its Arabidopsis ortholog, |FRAME: AT5G47720 AACT1|, it has a 6-fold higher catalytic efficiency &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; (k&lt;sub&gt;cat&lt;/sub&gt; of 503 +/- 26 vs. 87 +/- 5 min &lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt;) |CITS: [22332816]|. In Arabidopsis, this enzyme likely plays a major role in providing the necessary |FRAME: ACETOACETYL-COA acetoacetyl-CoA| for the cytosolic |FRAME: PWY-5910 mevalonate pathway| for isoprenoid production. Consistent with this hypothesis RNAi plants with reduced levels of AACT2 have root phytosterol profiles that differ both qualitatively and quantitatively from wild type plants and accompanying phenotypic abornmalities such as decreased cell number, decreased cell size, increased sterility, and reduced seed yield |CITS: [22332816]|. In addition, &lt;i&gt;aact2&lt;/i&gt; mutants have defects in embryogenesis and male gamete formation. The transcript expression pattern of AACT2 correlates with many of the observed phenotypic defects as high levels of AACT2 promoter-driven GUS expression can be detected in root tips, top stems, young leaves, and anthers. Its protein levels also seem to be higher that that of AACT1 in most parts of the plant |CITS: [22332816]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G48230-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="acetyl-CoA acetyltransferase" />
      <Property key="Synonym_2" value="acetoacetyl-coenyme A thiolase" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-33542, RXN-6800" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="2" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT5G48230.2" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT5G48230" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_22332816" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT5G47770-MONOMER" textLabel="AT5G47770-MONOMER" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q09152" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="600" centerY="775" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; gene &lt;i&gt;FPS1&lt;/i&gt; was shown to complement the mutant phenotype of the &lt;i&gt;Saccharomyces cerevisiae&lt;/i&gt; mutant strain CC25 which bears a leaky mutation (erg20) preventing the condensation of GPP with IPP to form FPP, resulting in a lethal phenotype |CITS:[7858223]|. The complementation experiment therefore demonstrates the ability of FPS to catalyze the conversion of GPP into FPP. Moreover, the &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; gene was also able to complement another &lt;i&gt;S. cerevisiae&lt;/i&gt; mutant strain (DD163) where the FPP synthase gene was fully disrupted, showing that the FPS1 catalyzes both IPP condensation steps |CITS:[7858223]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G47770-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT5G47770.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q09152" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Pid" value="CAA53433" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="geranyltranstransferase//dimethylallyltranstransferase" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-12473, RXN-12472" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="2" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT5G47770.1" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT5G47770" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_8631820" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_7858223" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT1G17050-MONOMER" textLabel="Solanesyl pyrophosphate synthase 2, chloroplastic" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q76FS5" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="750" centerY="775" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana SPS2&lt;/i&gt; gene was shown to encode a protein with polyprenyl diphosphate synthase activity |CITS:[15653808]|. This was assessed by phenotypical complementation of the &lt;i&gt;Schizosaccharomyces pombe&lt;/i&gt; mutant strains &amp;Delta;&lt;i&gt;dps1&lt;/i&gt; and &amp;Delta;&lt;i&gt;dls1&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[15653808]| (note that the recombinant protein was a 45-aminoacid mitochondrial targeting signal from Ppt1, which is known to be located in the mitochondria) and the purification and assay of a recombinant form of the protein |CITS:[12437513]|. |FRAME: ORG-5993| possesses two isozymes that catalyze the same reaction. |FRAME: AT1G17050| supplies the precursor of the plastoquinone side-chain, while |FRAME: AT1G78510| contributes to the biosynthesis of the ubiquinone side-chain |CITS: [15784989]|. Characterization of the enzyme &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; shows that |FRAME: AT1G17050| can use FPP or GGPP as substrates and catalyzes the formation of a variety of polyprenyl diphosphate products ranging from C&lt;sub&gt;25&lt;/sub&gt; (|FRAME:GERANYLGERANYL-PP|) to C&lt;sub&gt;45&lt;/sub&gt; (|FRAME: SOLANESYL-PYROPHOSPHATE|) (SPP) but the majority is released as SPP |CITS:[15784989]|, especially when |FRAME:GERANYLGERANYL-PP GGPP| is used as the substrate. |FRAME:GERANYLGERANYL-PP GGPP| is the preferred substrate over FPP for |FRAME: AT1G17050| |CITS:[15784989]| and |FRAME: AT1G78510||CITS: [12437513]|</Comment>
